# Design: V0.9 treasure identity, C1 to C2 in one day

## Problem
The six treasures were six differently named progress tokens:
identity never affected strategy. The user asked for distinct
abilities but warned against rule bloat after seeing the first
set.

## Approach
Matched-seed ladder (480 games per arm, seeds 300000+, V0.8 seat
schedule): A baseline, B two start reveals, C distinct abilities.
C1 implemented the abilities exactly as proposed (stacking
modifiers, private galea inspection, pugio Prestige). C2 was the
same-day simplification: one-line cards, at most one equipment
modifier per duel side, public galea reveal, pugio as a
tiebreaker.

## Key findings
- C1 drew 24.8% of games through a claim-recycling loop:
  attack modifiers stacked (gladius + pilum + paid challenge vs
  scutum alone) and pugio fed the claim economy. Claims doubled
  to 17.2 per game.
- C2 restored baseline claim flow (10.2 vs 9.5 per game) while
  abilities fired in ~98% of games. The collector edge fell from
  34% to 25%, which is the intent: set completion stopped being
  the only plan.
- B (reveal only) changed routing (35% of first explores target a
  revealed district) but no outcome measure; bots cannot price
  hidden information.

## Gotchas
- A duel-math rewrite briefly dropped the count-tier bonus for
  rules without abilities, silently changing the baseline arm.
  Caught by cross-run determinism (0/480 winners must repeat).
  Regression test added: test_baseline_keeps_its_count_tier_bonus_in_duels.
- The equipment RNG stream is used only by the district shuffle;
  drawing start reveals from it keeps every other stream
  bit-identical across arms on matched seeds.
- Abilities and count tiers are made mutually exclusive by
  validation (nonzero tiers + abilities = invalid rules).

